WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.220
NARRATOR: Exposure is not one brightness dial.

2
00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:08.057
NARRATOR: It is the combined result of
aperture, shutter speed, and ISO.

3
00:00:08.057 --> 00:00:13.641
NARRATOR: If one setting admits or records
less light, another may need to compensate.

4
00:00:13.641 --> 00:00:19.307
NARRATOR: The useful question is not only
whether the image is bright enough, but what

5
00:00:19.307 --> 00:00:21.195
visual trade-off each setting creates.

6
00:00:21.314 --> 00:00:26.454
NARRATOR: A wider aperture admits more light
and can produce shallower depth of field.

7
00:00:26.454 --> 00:00:31.630
NARRATOR: A narrower aperture admits less
light and can keep more of the scene in focus.

8
00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:37.473
NARRATOR: A fast shutter can freeze motion; a
slow shutter records motion across time.

9
00:00:37.473 --> 00:00:41.505
NARRATOR: These are creative decisions as well
as exposure decisions.

10
00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:46.712
NARRATOR: When depth of field and motion
already determine aperture and shutter speed,

11
00:00:46.712 --> 00:00:49.481
ISO can help reach a usable brightness.

12
00:00:49.481 --> 00:00:53.721
NARRATOR: Raising it can also make noise more
visible and reduce image quality.

13
00:00:53.721 --> 00:00:59.082
NARRATOR: Start from the look the photograph
needs, then balance the three controls instead

14
00:00:59.082 --> 00:01:02.145
of treating any one setting as automatically
correct.
